#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# interval_union

#% text
# `interval_union(i1, i2, ...)` gives the union of the intervals as one interval, overlapping pieces merged.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Real intervals and real centered intervals give an `interval` of disjoint pieces.

#% item
# When a centered interval with a complex centre is involved the result is the smallest centered interval that contains them all.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
interval_union(interval([1, 3]), interval([2, 4]))
#% output
#= interval([1, 4])

#% cell
interval_union(interval([-2, 0]), interval([1, 4]))
#% output
#= interval([-2, 0], [1, 4])

#% cell
interval_union(centered_interval(-1, 1/2), centered_interval(1, 1/2))
#% output
#= interval([-3/2, -1/2], [1/2, 3/2])

#% cell
interval_union(centered_interval(2 + 3*im, 1 + im), centered_interval(1 + im, 2 + 2*im))
#% output
#= centered_interval(1 + 3*im/2, 2 + 5*im/2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `interval`, `centered_interval`, `interval_member_q`
